{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Clearance: one payment, every boundary",
  "description": "A beginner-oriented code and test walkthrough for interview preparation. Trace a funded USD payment, then explain each boundary's guarantees, failures, rationale, and evidence. Describes current implementation and known proof gaps; no source changes.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "go.mod",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "1. Orient yourself: Go services, not a backend framework",
      "description": "Clearance is a learning project for asynchronous payment authorization. The backend uses Go's net/http, encoding/json, context, and testing packages, with pgx for PostgreSQL, kafka-go for Redpanda's Kafka API, go-redis for rate limits, and Prometheus for metrics. There is no Gin, ORM, or dependency-injection framework. React, TypeScript, Vite, and Vitest support the optional frontend.\n\nRead cmd/ as executable wiring, internal/ as implementation packages, migrations/ as database rules, and frontend/ as the demo UI. All four backend processes share PostgreSQL; these are not independently owned service databases. A Go interface describes required methods; postgres.Store satisfies service interfaces implicitly. Constructors receive that store, while tests can supply memory fakes.\n\nSay aloud: 'This project explores reliable asynchronous processing. The business rule is deliberately simple so I can study duplicate delivery and partial failure.' Why: this is the rationale supported by README.md, not a claim about the author's private decision history."
    },
    {
      "file": "cmd/transaction-service/main.go",
      "line": 22,
      "title": "2. Boot the HTTP process and learn the Go vocabulary",
      "description": "main opens the PostgreSQL pool and Redis limiter, constructs business services, and passes them into the HTTP router. http.Server calls Router.ServeHTTP in internal/httpapi/metrics.go; that wraps request metrics and calls serveHTTP in router.go. There is no hidden route discovery.\n\ncontext.Context carries cancellation/deadlines. signal.NotifyContext starts shutdown when the process receives a termination signal. defer schedules cleanup when a function returns. A goroutine waits for shutdown while the server handles HTTP. A receiver such as (s *Service) attaches a method to a type. Returning (value, error) makes failure explicit; fmt.Errorf with %w preserves an underlying error for errors.Is.\n\nGuarantee: dependencies and shutdown paths are explicit. Limit: /healthz returns process liveness, not proof that PostgreSQL, Redis, and Kafka can complete a payment. Inspect internal/health/server.go and router.go. Tests in internal/httpapi/metrics_test.go verify metrics exposure and route labels, not full dependency readiness.\n\nSay aloud: 'main wires infrastructure; handlers translate HTTP; services coordinate work; the PostgreSQL package owns SQL and atomic writes.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/funding.go",
      "line": 39,
      "title": "3. Establish the starting balance",
      "description": "Use one running example: account acct_123 already has 50,000 USD cents ($500), and requests a 12,550-cent ($125.50) payment. The demo funding route POST /accounts/{account_id}/deposits uses a separate funding bearer credential and an operator reason.\n\nCreateDeposit takes an account/currency advisory lock, writes an AUTHORIZED DEPOSIT transaction, credits the account, debits external-settlement, saves its idempotency response and audit action, and inserts FundsDeposited into the outbox in one database transaction. Funding takes effect synchronously; it does not wait for the risk service. The external reference uniqueness rule also prevents reusing a funding reference under another idempotency key.\n\nGuarantee: the supported deposit path commits its related writes together. Failure: a write error rolls back; an actual bank transfer is not verified. Tradeoff: a useful demo funding rail without settlement integration.\n\nEvidence: internal/postgres/funding_query_integration_test.go, TestCreateDepositIsBalancedIdempotentAndQueryable checks two entries, a zero total, idempotency, an event, an audit row, and querying. It does not simulate every deposit write failing.\n\nSay aloud: 'This is authenticated demo funding, not money arriving from a bank.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/lib/api.ts",
      "line": 53,
      "title": "4. Submit a payment and distinguish its identifiers",
      "description": "submitTransaction sends JSON to POST /transactions. For the example, the body is account_id=acct_123, merchant_id=merchant_123, amount_cents=12550, currency=USD. Headers carry Authorization: Bearer <configured value>, Idempotency-Key: idem-demo-1, and X-Correlation-ID: trace-demo-1.\n\nThe idempotency key identifies the client's logical operation across HTTP retries. transaction_id identifies the resulting business record. Each outbox event receives its own event_id, retained when that event is republished. correlation_id connects work for diagnosis. account_id becomes the Kafka partition key. These are different responsibilities.\n\nGuarantee: frontend helpers construct the API contract. Failure: fetch throws for network errors, while HTTP 4xx/5xx require checking response.ok. Frontend validation is convenience, not a security boundary; the server validates again. React state and the last twelve receipts are local, not the durable ledger.\n\nEvidence: frontend/test/lib/transaction.test.ts covers headers and payload validation; frontend/test/lib/api.test.ts covers API errors and authenticated reads. Those are helper tests, not full browser journeys.\n\nSay aloud: 'The retry key prevents duplicate requests; the event ID prevents duplicate consumer effects; the correlation ID is for tracing.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/httpapi/transactions.go",
      "line": 16,
      "title": "5. Cross the HTTP trust boundary",
      "description": "createTransaction checks the transaction bearer credential, asks the limiter, limits the body to the configured maximum (1 MiB by default), decodes JSON with unknown fields rejected, and validates or generates the correlation ID. It then calls transaction.Service.Create with a request and metadata.\n\nGuarantee: this endpoint gates work behind a static credential and performs boundary checks. Failure responses include 401 for missing/wrong credentials, 429 for rate limits, 400 for invalid requests, 409 for changed payload under an existing key, and generic 500 for unexpected errors. Errors are wrapped internally and translated into public responses centrally.\n\nTradeoff: simple deployment and tests, but no per-user identity or account ownership authorization. CORS only controls browser access; it is not authentication. The decoder makes one Decode call; do not describe this as exhaustive validation of all possible trailing input.\n\nEvidence: internal/httpapi/transactions_test.go: TestTransactionHandlerRequiresBearerToken, TestTransactionHandlerCreatesPendingTransaction, TestTransactionHandlerHidesInternalErrors. The success test asserts 202, PENDING, correlation propagation, and one outbox event using a fake store.\n\nSay aloud: '202 will mean durable acceptance, not risk approval or a completed payment.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/redislimiter/limiter.go",
      "line": 45,
      "title": "6. Redis controls admission, not balances",
      "description": "The rate-limit key is based on the remote IP, ignoring the client's ephemeral port. X-Forwarded-For is used only when explicitly trusted. A SHA-256-derived Redis key holds a counter. The Lua script atomically increments it and sets expiry when first created. The default is 60 requests in a one-minute window starting with the first increment.\n\nGuarantee: instances share the counter, and increment plus initial expiry is one Redis script. Failure: Redis errors fail the request closed with 500; over-limit requests receive 429. Shared IPs share limits, and fixed windows permit bursts around expiry. Polling status also consumes the API rate limit.\n\nTradeoff: Redis adds a request-path dependency, but an in-process counter would reset on restart and allow each API replica its own quota. The comment mentioning TxPipeline is stale: read the Lua script as the implementation.\n\nEvidence: internal/httpapi/transactions_test.go covers denial before creation, remote-port stripping, trusted forwarding, and masking limiter errors. These use a fake limiter; there is no dedicated Redis integration test here.\n\nSay aloud: 'Redis is for shared admission control; PostgreSQL remains the source of truth for transactions and balances.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/transaction/service.go",
      "line": 65,
      "title": "7. Make an HTTP retry return the same operation",
      "description": "Service.Create normalizes the business request, validates safe tokens and a positive integer amount, then hashes account, merchant, amount, and currency. An existing key with the same hash returns the saved creation response. A different hash returns ErrIdempotencyConflict. A new request gets a PENDING transaction and TransactionCreated event.\n\nGuarantee: a supported retry with the same key and normalized business payload reuses its original result. Failure: two callers may both initially find no key; the unique database key arbitrates the race. The losing write rolls back, and the service rereads the winner before deciding whether to replay or reject.\n\nTradeoff: idempotency needs retained database state and stable request hashing. The key is currently global, not scoped to a customer identity. Its response is a creation receipt: a retry can still return PENDING after processing has finished. Use GET to obtain current status. Correlation ID is not part of the business payload hash.\n\nEvidence: internal/transaction/service_test.go: TestServiceReplaysSameIdempotencyKeyAndRejectsPayloadMismatch and TestServiceReplaysSamePayloadAfterConcurrentIdempotencyInsert. The latter simulates the race using a fake; it does not run competing PostgreSQL transactions.\n\nSay aloud: 'A retry is safe because the key maps to one request fingerprint and one saved result.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/transaction.go",
      "line": 39,
      "title": "8. Commit the request and the next event together",
      "description": "Store.Create begins a PostgreSQL transaction, inserts the payment row, saves the idempotency record and response, inserts the outbox event, and commits. Deferred rollback cleans up any earlier failure. The HTTP handler sends 202 only after Service.Create returns successfully.\n\nGuarantee: these three durable records commit together or roll back together. Kafka is not involved in this database transaction. If the response is lost after commit, the client can retry with the same idempotency key. A commit/network error can leave the client uncertain; retrying resolves that uncertainty against stored state.\n\nWhy an outbox: writing PostgreSQL and then independently publishing to Kafka creates a failure gap between the two writes. An outbox saves the intent to publish in the same database commit, so a separate worker can recover it. Cost: another table, worker, and processing delay.\n\nEvidence: TestServiceCreatesPendingTransactionAndOutboxEvent in internal/transaction/service_test.go checks the service contract with a fake. There is no dedicated initial-creation PostgreSQL rollback test in the inspected suite; do not confuse that with the real PostgreSQL rollback tests for risk/ledger.\n\nSay aloud: 'Acceptance means the payment request and durable intent to process it exist, not that Kafka has already received it.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/outbox.go",
      "line": 12,
      "title": "9. Cross from PostgreSQL to Kafka",
      "description": "NextPending atomically selects a row with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED and marks it PROCESSING. It can reclaim PROCESSING rows untouched for five minutes. internal/outbox/publisher.go publishes the claimed event and only then marks it PUBLISHED. cmd/outbox-publisher/main.go drains work and normally polls every second.\n\nGuarantee: ordinary concurrent claims avoid selecting the same locked row; failed work remains represented in PostgreSQL. Failure: Kafka may accept an event and the process may crash before marking it published. Reclaiming then republishes it. A worker exceeding the reclaim interval can also overlap another worker. SKIP LOCKED is not a duplicate-free publication guarantee.\n\nA publish failure increments attempts and returns to PENDING or reaches DEAD_LETTERED (default three attempts). DEAD_LETTERED is a PostgreSQL state here, not a Kafka DLQ message. Progress then requires recovery.\n\nEvidence: internal/outbox/publisher_test.go covers success, retry exhaustion, draining, and stopping a drain on error. scripts/broker-failure-suite.sh exercises a real outage and audited requeue. Concurrent claims, stale reclamation, and publish-success/mark-failure are not directly covered by those unit tests.\n\nSay aloud: 'I close the lost-event gap by accepting possible duplicate delivery and making consumers idempotent.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/kafkabus/bus.go",
      "line": 18,
      "title": "10. Read the Kafka envelope and its ordering limits",
      "description": "TopicFor maps TransactionCreated to transactions.created, RiskEvaluated to risk.evaluated, and final outcomes to transactions.authorized or transactions.failed. Messages use the account ID as key, JSON as value, and event_id plus correlation_id headers. Risk and ledger each have their own consumer group on their input topic.\n\nA topic is a named stream. A partition is one ordered log within it. An offset is a message position in that partition; a group commit records progress. Hash partitioning routes an account consistently within a topic while the partition topology stays stable. It does not create global ordering across topics, and concurrent outbox publishing can differ from HTTP arrival order.\n\nThe writer requests all in-sync replica acknowledgments. Compose creates three partitions with replication factor one: RequireAll is not high availability on a single broker.\n\nEvidence: internal/kafkabus/bus_test.go: TestMessageSeparatesPartitionKeyFromStableEventIdentity, TestTopicForMapsEveryBusinessEvent, TestTopicWritersReuseConfiguredWriter. These verify construction/configuration, not all broker failure semantics.\n\nSay aloud: 'Partition keys organize delivery; stable event IDs identify replays. They must not be the same concept.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/risk/service.go",
      "line": 33,
      "title": "11. Evaluate risk without finalizing the payment",
      "description": "cmd/risk-service/main.go fetches messages through the shared consumer loop, extracts their event identity and source coordinates, and calls HandleTransactionCreated. The service decodes JSON, checks required fields, and calls domain.EvaluateRisk.\n\nThe rule is amount > 50,000 cents => HIGH and rejected; otherwise LOW and approved. Exactly 50,000 is LOW. Our 12,550-cent example passes. This is a fixed numerical demo rule; it does not convert currencies or implement a fraud model. The service constructs RiskEvaluated, hashes the incoming raw bytes, and asks the store to save its successor event with the consumed-event record.\n\nGuarantee: the risk decision is deterministic for the supplied amount. It is not final authorization: available funds have not yet been checked, and the payment row remains PENDING until the ledger stage. Malformed/incomplete events return errors and enter the consumer retry path.\n\nEvidence: internal/domain/risk_test.go, TestEvaluateRiskUsesSimpleAmountRule; internal/risk/service_test.go checks duplicate delivery, changed bytes under one event ID, and malformed JSON.\n\nSay aloud: 'Risk approval only passes one gate. The ledger still has to authorize against the account balance.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/consumer.go",
      "line": 57,
      "title": "12. Atomically remember that an event was processed",
      "description": "claimProcessedEvent inserts a row keyed by (consumer_name, event_id). On conflict it compares the payload SHA-256: identical bytes are a successful no-op; changed bytes under that identity are rejected. SaveConsumerOutbox performs this claim and inserts the RiskEvaluated outbox row in one PostgreSQL transaction.\n\nGuarantee: while the deduplication record is retained, replaying the same event ID and bytes does not repeat that consumer's committed effects. If the outbox insert fails, the processed-event claim rolls back too; a retry is not incorrectly dismissed as already complete. Different JSON formatting changes the raw-byte hash even if it represents the same object.\n\nTradeoff: this is effectively-once database effects for stable identity, not globally exactly-once messaging or deduplication of unrelated event IDs. It adds storage and retention rules. Consumer names separate deduplication namespaces.\n\nEvidence: internal/postgres/store_integration_test.go, TestSaveConsumerOutboxIsIdempotentAndAtomic uses real PostgreSQL, checks duplicate/conflicting deliveries, and forces a duplicate outbox ID to verify rollback of the processed-event claim.\n\nSay aloud: 'The receipt saying I processed an event must commit with the effects of processing it.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/ledger.go",
      "line": 14,
      "title": "13. Authorize against locked balance and write the ledger",
      "description": "internal/ledger/service.go validates the RiskEvaluated payload and valid approval/risk combinations. ProcessRiskEvaluated then begins a database transaction, claims the incoming event, locks the PENDING payment row, and checks its account, amount, and currency against the event. Approved risk decisions also take an account/currency advisory lock before summing ledger entries.\n\nFor the example, 50,000 available cents covers 12,550. The code inserts -12,550 for acct_123 and +12,550 for clearing, then sets AUTHORIZED and inserts TransactionAuthorized. The balance becomes 37,450 cents. A high-risk decision or insufficient funds produces FAILED and TransactionFailed without payment ledger entries. Insufficient funds is a business outcome, not a transient handler error to retry.\n\nGuarantee: supported balance-changing paths serialize on the same account/currency lock; all ledger-stage writes share one database commit. Tradeoff: serialization and summing history can limit a hot account's throughput. Business policy is partly in the PostgreSQL package to keep the transaction cohesive.\n\nSay aloud: 'The payment row lock prevents conflicting finalization of one payment; the account lock coordinates different payments spending the same balance.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/store_integration_test.go",
      "line": 141,
      "title": "14. Prove rollback rather than trusting test names",
      "description": "TestProcessRiskEvaluatedRollsBackStateWhenOutboxInsertFails installs a PostgreSQL trigger that rejects final-outcome outbox inserts. After processing fails, it checks that the payment is still PENDING, with no payment ledger entries and no processed-event claim. This is concrete proof of all-or-nothing ledger-stage behavior under that failure.\n\nTestProcessRiskEvaluatedProducesOneAtomicLedgerOutcome processes the same event twice and checks two ledger rows, one final event, and one processed-event row. Unit tests in internal/ledger/service_test.go cover insufficient funds and high risk using MemoryStore. That fake implements substantial ledger logic itself; passing unit tests alone cannot prove the real SQL or locking behavior.\n\nmigrations/001_init.sql prevents normal UPDATE/DELETE of ledger rows with a trigger and restricts duplicate transaction/account entries. Balanced pairs are constructed by application code; there is no general database zero-sum constraint shown. The unit test named CreatesImmutableLedgerEntries does not attempt an actual database mutation.\n\nGaps: no dedicated concurrent-spend regression or UPDATE/DELETE trigger test was found in the inspected suite. The integration helper resets public schema; run it only against a disposable test database.\n\nSay aloud: 'I distinguish tests of a fake's contract from tests of the actual persistence guarantees.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/state/useConsole.ts",
      "line": 104,
      "title": "15. Read the final result independently of event publication",
      "description": "After a successful POST the console adds a local PENDING receipt. useConsole immediately reads pending transaction IDs and repeats reads roughly every 1.5 seconds. GET /transactions/{id} passes through the authenticated router to QueryService.Get and postgres.GetTransaction, which reads the durable transaction row.\n\nGuarantee: the final display is based on PostgreSQL status rather than the frontend's risk preview. The ledger commit can make AUTHORIZED visible before the final outbox event reaches Kafka. No extra consumer is required to update the UI. Creation retries still replay the original creation response; this GET supplies current status.\n\nFailure: failed reads preserve the receipt and retry silently. A stopped pipeline or expired credential can leave the UI showing PENDING without explaining progress; requests also consume the rate limit. Tradeoff: polling is simple but adds latency and read traffic. Bearer credentials stay in React memory; the API URL is persisted.\n\nEvidence: internal/httpapi/funding_query_test.go checks read credential scopes; internal/transaction/query_test.go checks querying/pagination with a fake; frontend/test/lib/api.test.ts checks the authenticated GET helper. There is no polling lifecycle component test in the inspected frontend suite.\n\nSay aloud: 'The preview predicts risk; the database tells me the actual outcome.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/consumer/loop.go",
      "line": 35,
      "title": "16. Revisit failure: retries, dead letters, and an offset gap",
      "description": "RunLoop fetches without automatically committing, retries the handler (default three attempts; linearly increasing delays), then either commits after success or asks deadletter.Recorder to preserve the exact message and publish it to the dead-letter topic. Recorder persists source topic/partition/offset, key, headers, bytes, and bounded error text before publication.\n\nEvidence: TestRunLoopRetriesDeadLettersAndCommits in internal/consumer/loop_test.go covers retry exhaustion followed by a successful DLQ move and commit. internal/deadletter/recorder_test.go checks preservation and persistence/publication/mark errors separately. It does not prove every combined failure path.\n\nKnown gap: if DLQ handling fails, continue fetches another message. FetchMessage does not rewind just because the previous offset was uncommitted. Committing a later message in the same partition also commits past earlier offsets (see kafka-go Reader.CommitMessages). If both handling and durable DLQ persistence failed, this can skip unresolved work. A missing regression is two same-partition messages where the first fails handling and DLQ storage, then the second succeeds. Do not repeat the comment's unconditional retry guarantee.\n\nSay aloud: 'Database deduplication handles replay, but safe offset advancement also needs to preserve unresolved earlier messages.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/operations/service.go",
      "line": 68,
      "title": "17. Recovery has identity, audit, and retention boundaries",
      "description": "clearance-admin supports inspecting and replaying durable dead letters, requeueing DEAD_LETTERED outbox rows, and pruning processed-event records. Replay checks OPEN state, its allowed time window, and whether the event was already processed, then republishes the original key, headers, and bytes with an audited attempt. Republished means sent again, not successfully processed.\n\nGuarantee: supported recovery preserves event identity and records operator intent. Failure: an unchanged malformed payload will fail again; fix the cause before replay. Publication and the final replay audit update are separate operations, so replay is not duplicate-free either. Outbox requeue resets attempts and records an action in one database transaction.\n\nRetention defaults to 30 days, replay to 14; maintenance rejects retention shorter than the replay window. It prunes bounded batches. Deduplication is only as long-lived as the stored record and replay assumptions; arbitrary historical broker replays are not made safe by these defaults.\n\nEvidence: internal/operations/service_test.go covers exact replay, audited attempts, invalid/expired/processed replay and publish failures. internal/postgres/operations_integration_test.go exercises durability and competing replay claims. internal/maintenance/processed_events_test.go covers unsafe retention and bounded pruning.\n\nSay aloud: 'Recovery is an explicit, audited operation; retrying forever is not the recovery policy.'"
    },
    {
      "file": "scripts/broker-failure-suite.sh",
      "line": 137,
      "title": "18. Rehearse the whole story and choose the right proof",
      "description": "Read this script as an executable system story: it funds an account, authorizes a payment, republishes the original event and checks unchanged effects, stops Redpanda until an outbox row is DEAD_LETTERED, restarts and requeues it, then injects malformed bytes and inspects the durable DLQ. It creates an isolated Compose project and removes that project's volumes; do not run it against shared infrastructure.\n\nVerification layers: go test ./... covers fast Go tests; frontend npm test covers helper contracts; integration-tagged PostgreSQL tests exercise real SQL with CLEARANCE_TEST_DATABASE_URL pointing only at a disposable database; the shell suite exercises real broker boundaries. These layers prove different things.\n\nObservability: optional /metrics exposes request/consumer/outbox outcomes and backlog gauges; deployment files provision Prometheus and Grafana. Correlation IDs aid investigation but are not a complete distributed tracing system. Low-cardinality labels avoid one metrics series per account or transaction. /healthz is only liveness.\n\nPractice aloud without reading: trace the 12,550-cent payment; identify the three business-stage database commits; explain lost HTTP responses, lost broker acknowledgments, duplicate events, insufficient funds, and failed DLQ storage. For every guarantee, open the test and name exactly what its assertions prove. Finish by explaining why a synchronous single service would be simpler for this business rule and why this repo deliberately explores asynchronous reliability."
    }
  ]
}
